ed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi}{8}, \\frac{9\\pi}{8}, \\frac{13\\pi}{8}, \\frac{17\\pi}{8}.$" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523039 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523026 1071 "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-4),\\,y=3{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical." 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523026 1072 #uuid "1b45aef8-5aba-59a4-a0c0-7ae74ccf1ed7" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523026 1074 "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9({t}^{2}-2)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{2}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{2}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523027 1075 #edb/ref 17592186523080 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523027 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523027 1077 "field-1" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523027 1078 #edb/ref 17592186523080 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523027 1079 #uuid "20ef8185-3b01-5cc2-a040-a6ce07a57c3b" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523028 1080 "\\frac{10\\pi}{9}" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523028 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523028 1083 #uuid "23126074-a8ae-566b-ad2a-40e0588f8cb5" 13194139535616 17592186045443 true] [17592186523029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523055 13194139535616 17592186045443